</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Research on accelerating attention broadly falls into two categories: (i) <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">lossless</span> system-level optimizations that preserve exact outputs, and (ii) <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">approximate</span> algorithm that trade accuracy for efficiency. Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes representative approaches.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Lossless methods.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">FlashAttention and its successors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., 2023</a>)</cite> reduce memory traffic by fusing attention kernels and tiling reads/writes to on-chip SRAM, achieving substantial GPU speedups without altering outputs. These approaches are <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">lossless</span> but depend heavily on GPU memory hierarchies and I/O architectures.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Approximate methods.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, many lines of work accelerate attention or reduce memory by introducing approximations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Linear-attention</span> replaces the quadratic softmax kernel with linear-time kernel approximations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Katharopoulos et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choromanski et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sparse-attention</span> imposes predefined or learned sparsity patterns, e.g., Sparse Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Child et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, Longformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beltagy et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, Reformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kitaev et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pruning</span> removes parameters based on importance criteria.</p>
<ul id="S2.I1.i3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unstructured pruning</span> targets individual weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar &amp; Alistarh, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, effective in reducing parameters but hard to accelerate on GPUs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semi-structured pruning</span> (e.g., <math id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\!:\!M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">:</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\!:\!M</annotation></semantics></math> patterns) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mishra et al., 2021</a>)</cite> enables GPU acceleration on NVIDIA’s Ampere GPUs but restricts density flexibility.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Structured pruning</span> removes entire channels/heads <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">van der Ouderaa et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ashkboos et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, more deployment-friendly but often with higher accuracy loss.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low-rank pruning</span> approximates weight matrices by decomposing them into lower-rank components <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, reducing both parameters and compute. While hardware-friendly, it introduces approximation error unless the true rank is low.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quantization</span> compresses parameters/activations into low-precision formats <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frantar et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, achieving strong memory savings but inevitably introducing errors at low bit-widths.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These methods can deliver large efficiency gains but are inherently <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not lossless</span>, and usually require careful finetuning to mitigate quality drops.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.01718v1/bd_illustration.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/367;" width="548" height="367" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Illustration of BD Attention (<span id="S3.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BDA</span>) using the QK projection as an example (VO is analogous).
BDA consists of two stages:
(a) <span id="S3.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BD Attention Preparation</span> (Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.4 BD for Multi-Head Attention ‣ 3 Method ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), performed offline <span id="S3.F1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">once</span> during model deployment, where the projection matrices are transformed via Basis Decomposition;
(b) <span id="S3.F1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BD Attention Inference</span> (Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3.4 BD for Multi-Head Attention ‣ 3 Method ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) saves <math id="S3.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{h}/d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{h}/d</annotation></semantics></math> in both parameters and computation, while preserving exact outputs.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Basis Decomposition</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given two rectangular matrices <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{V}^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{V}^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;\min(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;\min(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>, their product matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}=\vec{U}\vec{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}=\vec{U}\vec{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> has rank at most <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Our goal is to develop a general decomposition method that expresses <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}=f(\mathbf{M}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{M}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}=f(\mathbf{M}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{M}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, where the function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{M}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{M}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{M}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{M}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> has lower computational cost than the original multiplication <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{U}\vec{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{U}\vec{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We assume <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> has rank <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> without loss of generality. In this case, there exist exactly <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> linearly independent rows in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define a matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}=[\mathbf{b}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{b}_{r}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}=[\mathbf{b}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{b}_{r}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is one of these linearly independent rows. Thus, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math> forms a basis of the row space of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any row <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{i}\in\mathbf{W}\setminus\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>𝐁</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{i}\in\mathbf{W}\setminus\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, it can be written as a linear combination of the basis vectors in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{i}=\sum_{j=1}^{r}c_{ij}\mathbf{b}_{j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{i}=\sum_{j=1}^{r}c_{ij}\mathbf{b}_{j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">which follows directly from the fact that the row space of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by the <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> basis vectors in <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. Collecting all such coefficients <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> forms a coefficient matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}\in\mathbb{R}^{(m-r)\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}\in\mathbb{R}^{(m-r)\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each row contains the weights for reconstructing a non-basis row of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> as a linear combination of the basis vectors in <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. With <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, the original matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> can be fully reconstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This forms an alternative representation of the low-rank matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math>, distinct from the traditional low-rank multiplication <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}\mathbf{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐔𝐕</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}\mathbf{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. We refer to this representation as <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Basis Decomposition (BD)</span>, encompassing both the decomposition <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}\rightarrow(\mathbf{B},\mathbf{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}\rightarrow(\mathbf{B},\mathbf{C})</annotation></semantics></math> and its reconstruction <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{B},\mathbf{C})\rightarrow\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐖</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{B},\mathbf{C})\rightarrow\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use the term <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BD</span> for general reference, and specify <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BD decomposition/reconstruction</span> only when emphasizing the decomposition/reconstruction process.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory cost of BD.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">BD stores two matrices: the basis matrix <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> and the coefficient matrix <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}\in\mathbb{R}^{(m-r)\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}\in\mathbb{R}^{(m-r)\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>. The total memory cost is <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(m+n-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(m+n-r)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is strictly smaller than the full matrix size <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mn</annotation></semantics></math> and low rank matrices size <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(m+n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(m+n)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;\min(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;\min(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, the traditional low-rank representation <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}=\mathbf{U}\mathbf{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐔𝐕</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}=\mathbf{U}\mathbf{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> requires <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(m+n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(m+n)</annotation></semantics></math> parameters and is only more compact than the full matrix when <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;\frac{mn}{m+n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;\frac{mn}{m+n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Computational cost of BD reconstruction.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reconstructing <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> from BD involves computing <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math> and inserting the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> basis rows. This requires <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2r(m-r)n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2r(m-r)n</annotation></semantics></math> floating-point operations (FLOPs). In comparison, the traditional reconstruction <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}\mathbf{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐔𝐕</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}\mathbf{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> costs <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2rmn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2rmn</annotation></semantics></math> FLOPs. Therefore, BD reconstruction is computationally more efficient for any <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;\min(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;\min(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Selection of Basis</h3>

<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Almost Sure Full Rank of Random Matrices)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times r</annotation></semantics></math> real random matrix. Suppose the entries of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math>
are drawn from a probability measure <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{r^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{r^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>
that is absolutely continuous with respect to the Lebesgue measure <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> has full rank (<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{rank}(W)=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{rank}(W)=r</annotation></semantics></math>) with probability 1.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Almost Sure Full Rank of Random Matrices). ‣ 3.2 Selection of Basis ‣ 3 Method ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> (proof in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proof of Almost Sure Full Rank of Random Matrices Theorem ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>) states that if a matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> has entries drawn from a distribution that is absolutely continuous with respect to the Lebesgue measure, then <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is full rank with probability 1. In practical terms, this applies to any matrix with random noise of arbitrary (non-degenerate) scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, when <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated as a product of noised matrices <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{U}\vec{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{U}\vec{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can safely assume in practice that any selection of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> rows from <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}</annotation></semantics></math> forms a matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> that is full-rank. This situation is common in practice—for example, when <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{V}</annotation></semantics></math> are weight matrices learned via stochastic gradient descent (SGD) in neural networks, where random initialization and noisy updates introduce sufficient perturbations. As a result, any <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times r</annotation></semantics></math> submatrix of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, formed by choosing <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> columns, is full rank with probability 1, resulting that the rows of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent. This allows us to <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">freely choose</span> any <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> rows (or <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> columns) as a valid basis for BD reconstruction, without requiring explicit rank analysis or rotating.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, PIFA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> can be regarded as a special case of BD: it selects basis rows via QR factorization with column pivoting, approximating the most numerically independent directions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Businger &amp; Golub, 1971</a>)</cite>. While this is useful in rare rank-deficient or ill-conditioned settings, such guarantees are unnecessary in typical neural networks where noise ensures full rank. Hence, BD offers a more flexible and efficient basis selection strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, we find that choosing contiguous rows/columns, such as the first‑<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> or last‑<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> rows/columns, offers significant efficiency advantages. It minimizes the I/O overhead during reconstruction by avoiding scattered memory writes/reads on modern hardware such as GPUs. We adopt this strategy as the standard basis selection method for BD in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the identity matrix. The following identities hold for the four types of BD:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E2X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(1) row \&amp; first: }\mathbf{W}\equiv\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{I}\\
\mathbf{C}\end{bmatrix}\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>(1) row &amp; first: </mtext><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>𝐈</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>𝐂</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mi>𝐁</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(1) row \&amp; first: }\mathbf{W}\equiv\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{I}\\
\mathbf{C}\end{bmatrix}\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle,\quad\text{(2) row \&amp; last: }\mathbf{W}\equiv\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{C}\\
\mathbf{I}\end{bmatrix}\mathbf{B}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>(2) row &amp; last: </mtext><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>𝐂</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>𝐈</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle,\quad\text{(2) row \&amp; last: }\mathbf{W}\equiv\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{C}\\
\mathbf{I}\end{bmatrix}\mathbf{B},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E2Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(3) column \&amp; first: }\mathbf{W}\equiv\mathbf{B}[\mathbf{I},\mathbf{C}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>(3) column &amp; first: </mtext><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>≡</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(3) column \&amp; first: }\mathbf{W}\equiv\mathbf{B}[\mathbf{I},\mathbf{C}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle,\quad\text{(4) column \&amp; last: }\mathbf{W}\equiv\mathbf{B}[\mathbf{C},\mathbf{I}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>(4) column &amp; last: </mtext><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>≡</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle,\quad\text{(4) column \&amp; last: }\mathbf{W}\equiv\mathbf{B}[\mathbf{C},\mathbf{I}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">While the theoretical reconstruction is exact, numerical residuals may arise in practice due to finite-precision arithmetic or ill-conditioned basis matrices. To mitigate this, we compare the reconstruction errors from the first- and last-<math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> basis candidates and retain the one with the smaller Frobenius norm residual. The full procedure for <span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">row-based</span> Basis Decomposition is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#alg4" title="Algorithm 4 ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (BD decomposition) and Algorithm <a href="#alg5" title="Algorithm 5 ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (BD reconstruction), where a subset of rows is selected as the basis. The <span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">column-based</span> variant can be formulated analogously and is omitted here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">By losslessly replacing standard low-rank matrix multiplication with a more compact and computationally efficient alternative, BD is broadly applicable to scenarios involving low-rank multiplications. This includes applications such as neural network inference and data compression.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>BD for Linear Layer</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The linear layer is the most common component in neural networks. Given an input vector <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a standard linear layer computes the output <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math> using a weight matrix <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbf{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}\times d_{\text{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbf{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}\times d_{\text{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=\mathbf{x}\overline{\mathbf{W}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=\mathbf{x}\overline{\mathbf{W}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To reduce parameter count and computational cost, many recent works adopt low-rank approximations of the weight matrix. A common approach is to factorize <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbf{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbf{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbf{W}}\approx\mathbf{U}\mathbf{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>𝐔𝐕</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbf{W}}\approx\mathbf{U}\mathbf{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;\min(d_{\text{in}},d_{\text{out}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;\min(d_{\text{in}},d_{\text{out}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The resulting low-rank layer becomes:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=(\mathbf{x}\mathbf{U})\mathbf{V}^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱𝐔</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=(\mathbf{x}\mathbf{U})\mathbf{V}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which reduces both the number of parameters from <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{in}}d_{\text{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{in}}d_{\text{out}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2r(d_{\text{in}}+d_{\text{out}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2r(d_{\text{in}}+d_{\text{out}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Such low-rank structures appear in various domains in deep learning: (1) <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">low-rank pruning</span>, where pretrained weight matrices are compressed post hoc via SVD-like approximations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jaiswal et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saha et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaushal et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qinsi et al., </a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sakr &amp; Khailany, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren &amp; Zhu, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hajimolahoseini et al., 2022</a>)</cite>; (2) <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">low-rank training</span>, where the weight matrices are parameterized as low-rank products throughout training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khodak et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schotthöfer et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kamalakara et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Savostianova et al., 2023</a>)</cite>; (3) <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">low-rank + sparse hybridization</span>, which combines sparsity and low-rank approximations for improved performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang &amp; Papyan, 2025</a>)</cite>; and (4) <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LoRA-style fine-tuning</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lialin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>, where a low-rank adaptation is injected into frozen models for efficient parameter updates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Since Basis Decomposition (BD) operates directly on the product <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}\mathbf{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐔𝐕</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}\mathbf{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, it can be seamlessly applied to all these cases in a lossless and hardware-efficient manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To replace the low-rank linear layer with a BD layer, we decompose the weight product <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}=\mathbf{U}\mathbf{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐔𝐕</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}=\mathbf{U}\mathbf{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> using column-based BD. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> be the first-<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> column and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times(d_{\text{out}}-r)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times(d_{\text{out}}-r)}</annotation></semantics></math> the coefficient matrix. The BD layer computes the output in two steps:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}\mathbf{B},\quad\mathbf{y}\leftarrow[\mathbf{h},\mathbf{h}\mathbf{C}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝐱𝐁</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡𝐂</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}\mathbf{B},\quad\mathbf{y}\leftarrow[\mathbf{h},\mathbf{h}\mathbf{C}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The last-<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> version is similar. For any <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;\min(d_{\text{in}},d_{\text{out}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;\min(d_{\text{in}},d_{\text{out}})</annotation></semantics></math>, BD achieves strictly lower FLOPs and memory cost than the original low-rank layer, reduced by <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{r}{d_{\text{in}}+d_{\text{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{r}{d_{\text{in}}+d_{\text{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math> relative to the original.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>BD for Multi-Head Attention</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-head attention (MHA) is a central component in Transformer-based architectures, widely adopted in large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and vision-language models (VLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dosovitskiy et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. The comparison between BD Attention and MHA is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Method ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by reviewing the standard multi-head attention (MHA) mechanism. Let <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> be the input (embedding) dimension, <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of attention heads, <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> be the dimension per head, <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be the input sequence length, and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the attention input. MHA produces queries, keys and values (<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q},\mathbf{K},\mathbf{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times nd_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q},\mathbf{K},\mathbf{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times nd_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>) by three projection matrices (<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{q},\mathbf{W}_{k},\mathbf{W}_{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times nd_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{q},\mathbf{W}_{k},\mathbf{W}_{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times nd_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}=\mathbf{X}\mathbf{W}_{q},\quad\mathbf{K}=\mathbf{X}\mathbf{W}_{k},\quad\mathbf{V}=\mathbf{X}\mathbf{W}_{v}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐗𝐖</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐗𝐖</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐗𝐖</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}=\mathbf{X}\mathbf{W}_{q},\quad\mathbf{K}=\mathbf{X}\mathbf{W}_{k},\quad\mathbf{V}=\mathbf{X}\mathbf{W}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q},\mathbf{K},\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q},\mathbf{K},\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math> are divided into <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> heads for MHA:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[\mathbf{Q}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{Q}_{n}]=\mathbf{Q},\quad[\mathbf{K}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{K}_{n}]=\mathbf{K},\quad[\mathbf{V}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{V}_{n}]=\mathbf{V}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐊</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[\mathbf{Q}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{Q}_{n}]=\mathbf{Q},\quad[\mathbf{K}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{K}_{n}]=\mathbf{K},\quad[\mathbf{V}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{V}_{n}]=\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{O}_{i}=\text{softmax}(\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{i}\mathbf{K}_{i}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{h}}})\mathbf{V}_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐎</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{O}_{i}=\text{softmax}(\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{i}\mathbf{K}_{i}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{h}}})\mathbf{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{Y}=[\mathbf{O}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{O}_{n}]\mathbf{W}_{o}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐎</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐎</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{Y}=[\mathbf{O}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{O}_{n}]\mathbf{W}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i},\mathbf{K}_{i},\mathbf{V}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i},\mathbf{K}_{i},\mathbf{V}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the respective query, key, and value vectors for the <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th head, and <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{o}\in\mathbb{R}^{nd_{h}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{o}\in\mathbb{R}^{nd_{h}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the output projection matrix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We reformulate multi-head attention (MHA).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E10X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{O}_{i}\mathbf{W}_{o}^{i}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\text{softmax}(\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{i}\mathbf{K}_{i}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{h}}})\mathbf{V}_{i}\mathbf{W}_{o}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>𝐎</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{O}_{i}\mathbf{W}_{o}^{i}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\text{softmax}(\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{i}\mathbf{K}_{i}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{h}}})\mathbf{V}_{i}\mathbf{W}_{o}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E10Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\text{softmax}\left(\frac{\mathbf{X}(\mathbf{W}_{q}^{i}{(\mathbf{W}_{k}^{\,i})}^{\!\top})\mathbf{X}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{h}}}\right)\mathbf{X}(\mathbf{W}_{v}^{i}\mathbf{W}_{o}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\text{softmax}\left(\frac{\mathbf{X}(\mathbf{W}_{q}^{i}{(\mathbf{W}_{k}^{\,i})}^{\!\top})\mathbf{X}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{h}}}\right)\mathbf{X}(\mathbf{W}_{v}^{i}\mathbf{W}_{o}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E10Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{W}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{W}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\rightarrow[\mathbf{W}_{q}^{1},\dots,\mathbf{W}_{q}^{n}],\quad\mathbf{W}_{k}\rightarrow[\mathbf{W}_{k}^{1},\dots,\mathbf{W}_{k}^{n}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\rightarrow[\mathbf{W}_{q}^{1},\dots,\mathbf{W}_{q}^{n}],\quad\mathbf{W}_{k}\rightarrow[\mathbf{W}_{k}^{1},\dots,\mathbf{W}_{k}^{n}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E10Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{W}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{W}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10Xc.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\rightarrow[\mathbf{W}_{v}^{1},\dots,\mathbf{W}_{v}^{n}],\quad\mathbf{W}_{o}\rightarrow\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{W}_{o}^{1}\\
\vdots\\
\mathbf{W}_{o}^{n}\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\rightarrow[\mathbf{W}_{v}^{1},\dots,\mathbf{W}_{v}^{n}],\quad\mathbf{W}_{o}\rightarrow\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{W}_{o}^{1}\\
\vdots\\
\mathbf{W}_{o}^{n}\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{q}^{i},\mathbf{W}_{k}^{i},\mathbf{W}_{v}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{q}^{i},\mathbf{W}_{k}^{i},\mathbf{W}_{v}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S3.SS4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th <em id="S3.SS4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vertical slice</em> of the corresponding weight matrices, and <math id="S3.SS4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{o}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{h}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{o}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{h}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S3.SS4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th <em id="S3.SS4.p9.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">horizontal slice</em> of <math id="S3.SS4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>. Both <math id="S3.SS4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{W}_{q}^{i}{\mathbf{W}_{k}^{i}}^{\top})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{W}_{q}^{i}{\mathbf{W}_{k}^{i}}^{\top})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{W}_{v}^{i}\mathbf{W}_{o}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{W}_{v}^{i}\mathbf{W}_{o}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are a matrix with shape <math id="S3.SS4.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> multiply a matrix with shape <math id="S3.SS4.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{h}\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{h}\times d</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S3.SS4.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{h}&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{h}&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>, this reveals a key insight:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS4.p10.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="56.46" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 56.46" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,56.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#BFBFBF;" fill="#BFBFBF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 56.46 L 550 56.46 L 550 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 1.97 L 1.97 54.49 L 548.03 54.49 L 548.03 1.97 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.47)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:1.89em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="28.9" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 26.21)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS4.p10.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S3.SS4.p10.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p10.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Each head’s QK and VO computation <span id="S3.SS4.p10.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">inherently are low-rank matrix multiplications</span>.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We can apply Basis Decomposition (BD) <span id="S3.SS4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">offline during model preparation</span> to compress these projection weight. Taking QK as an example (VO in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B BD for VO in MHA ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>), we decompose the weight product <math id="S3.SS4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{W}_{q}^{i}{\mathbf{W}_{k}^{i}}^{\top})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{W}_{q}^{i}{\mathbf{W}_{k}^{i}}^{\top})</annotation></semantics></math> using column-based BD. Let <math id="S3.SS4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the first-<math id="S3.SS4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> column (last-<math id="S3.SS4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is similar) and <math id="S3.SS4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{h}\times(d-d_{h})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{h}\times(d-d_{h})}</annotation></semantics></math> the coefficient matrix, we can convert the attention score into expression of BD:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E11X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{attn\_score}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>attn_score</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{attn\_score}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{Q}_{i}\mathbf{K}_{i}^{\top}=\mathbf{X}(\mathbf{W}_{q}^{i}{(\mathbf{W}_{k}^{i})}^{\top})\mathbf{X}^{\top}=\mathbf{X}(\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{i}[\mathbf{I},\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{i}])\mathbf{X}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{Q}_{i}\mathbf{K}_{i}^{\top}=\mathbf{X}(\mathbf{W}_{q}^{i}{(\mathbf{W}_{k}^{i})}^{\top})\mathbf{X}^{\top}=\mathbf{X}(\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{i}[\mathbf{I},\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{i}])\mathbf{X}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="5" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E11Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(\mathbf{X}\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{i})([\mathbf{I},\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{i}]\mathbf{X}^{\top})=(\mathbf{X}\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{i})(\mathbf{X}_{:,\,1:d_{h}}^{\top}+\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{i}\mathbf{X}_{:,\,d_{h}:d}^{\top})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗𝐁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗𝐁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mi>𝐗</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(\mathbf{X}\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{i})([\mathbf{I},\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{i}]\mathbf{X}^{\top})=(\mathbf{X}\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{i})(\mathbf{X}_{:,\,1:d_{h}}^{\top}+\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{i}\mathbf{X}_{:,\,d_{h}:d}^{\top})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E11Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{Q}_{i}^{\prime}{\mathbf{K}_{i}^{\prime}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{Q}_{i}^{\prime}{\mathbf{K}_{i}^{\prime}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E11Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{Q}_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{Q}_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11Xc.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leftarrow\mathbf{X}\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leftarrow\mathbf{X}\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E11Xd" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11Xd.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathbf{K}_{i}^{\prime}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathbf{K}_{i}^{\prime}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11Xd.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leftarrow\mathbf{X}_{:,\,1:d_{h}}^{\top}+\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{i}\mathbf{X}_{:,\,d_{h}:d}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mi>𝐗</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leftarrow\mathbf{X}_{:,\,1:d_{h}}^{\top}+\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{i}\mathbf{X}_{:,\,d_{h}:d}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p13.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{X}\rightarrow\big[\,\mathbf{X}_{:,\,1:d_{h}},\mathbf{X}_{:,\,d_{h}:d}\,\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}\rightarrow\big[\,\mathbf{X}_{:,\,1:d_{h}},\mathbf{X}_{:,\,d_{h}:d}\,\big]</annotation></semantics></math> partitions <math id="S3.SS4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> into its first <math id="S3.SS4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and the remaining <math id="S3.SS4.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-d_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-d_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> columns. By aligning all head’s BD to either first-<math id="S3.SS4.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> or last-<math id="S3.SS4.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, we avoid calculating each head’s Q and K projection separately, thereby reducing I/O overhead. The choice between first-<math id="S3.SS4.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and last-<math id="S3.SS4.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> columns is determined by comparing the average residuals across all heads (Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.4 BD for Multi-Head Attention ‣ 3 Method ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). This alignment allows Equation <a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.4 BD for Multi-Head Attention ‣ 3 Method ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, which computes QK for a single head, to be reformulated to compute QK for all heads simultaneously:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E12X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}\leftarrow\mathbf{X}\mathbf{B}_{qk},\quad\text{where}\;\mathbf{B}_{qk}\leftarrow[\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{1},\dots,\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>𝐗𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where</mtext><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}\leftarrow\mathbf{X}\mathbf{B}_{qk},\quad\text{where}\;\mathbf{B}_{qk}\leftarrow[\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{1},\dots,\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{n}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E12Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\displaystyle\mathbf{K}^{\prime}}\leftarrow{[\mathbf{X}_{:,\,1:d_{h}}]}^{\times n}+\mathbf{X}_{:,\,d_{h}:d}\mathbf{C}_{qk},\quad\text{where}\;\mathbf{C}_{qk}\leftarrow[{(\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{1})}^{\top},\dots,{(\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{n})}^{\top}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐊</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>where</mtext><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\displaystyle\mathbf{K}^{\prime}}\leftarrow{[\mathbf{X}_{:,\,1:d_{h}}]}^{\times n}+\mathbf{X}_{:,\,d_{h}:d}\mathbf{C}_{qk},\quad\text{where}\;\mathbf{C}_{qk}\leftarrow[{(\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{1})}^{\top},\dots,{(\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{n})}^{\top}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p15" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p15.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{[\mathbf{X}_{:,\,1:d_{h}}]}^{\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{[\mathbf{X}_{:,\,1:d_{h}}]}^{\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> means repeat matrix <math id="S3.SS4.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> times along second dimension. VO projection could be similarly processed with BD row-based (Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B BD for VO in MHA ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). We compare the original MHA inference (Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.4 BD for Multi-Head Attention ‣ 3 Method ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) with MHA BD inference (Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3.4 BD for Multi-Head Attention ‣ 3 Method ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). The only differences (highlighted in <span id="S3.SS4.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red</span>) are calculations of keys <math id="S3.SS4.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and values <math id="S3.SS4.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, in which BD uses smaller size matrices for multiplication. A discussion on the impact of positional embeddings on BD is provided in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Effect of Positional Embedding on BD ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.SS4.fig1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_figure_panel ltx_float_algorithm ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top ltx_framed ltx_framed_top" style="width:178.9pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> MHA Inference</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
                
                
               Weight <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times nd_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times nd_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times nd_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times nd_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times nd_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times nd_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{o}\in\mathbb{R}^{nd_{h}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{o}\in\mathbb{R}^{nd_{h}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>; Input <math id="alg1.l0.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times nd_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times nd_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
                
                
               <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}\leftarrow\mathbf{X}\mathbf{W}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>𝐗𝐖</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}\leftarrow\mathbf{X}\mathbf{W}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
                
                
               <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}\leftarrow\mathbf{X}\mathbf{W}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐊</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>𝐗𝐖</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}\leftarrow\mathbf{X}\mathbf{W}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
                
                
               <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}\leftarrow\mathbf{X}\mathbf{W}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>𝐗𝐖</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}\leftarrow\mathbf{X}\mathbf{W}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
                
                
               <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbf{Q}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{Q}_{n}]\leftarrow\mathbf{Q},\;[\mathbf{K}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{K}_{n}]\leftarrow\mathbf{K},\;[\mathbf{V}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{V}_{n}]\leftarrow\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝐊</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbf{Q}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{Q}_{n}]\leftarrow\mathbf{Q},\;[\mathbf{K}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{K}_{n}]\leftarrow\mathbf{K},\;[\mathbf{V}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{V}_{n}]\leftarrow\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
                
                
               <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{O}_{i}\leftarrow\text{softmax}(\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{i}\mathbf{K}_{i}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{h}}})\mathbf{V}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐎</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{O}_{i}\leftarrow\text{softmax}(\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{i}\mathbf{K}_{i}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{h}}})\mathbf{V}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
                
                
               <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}\leftarrow[\mathbf{O}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{O}_{n}]\mathbf{W}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐎</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐎</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}\leftarrow[\mathbf{O}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{O}_{n}]\mathbf{W}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6a" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
                
                
               <math id="alg1.l6a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_figure_panel ltx_float_algorithm ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top ltx_framed ltx_framed_top" style="width:178.9pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> BD Attention Inference</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
                
                
               Weight <math id="alg2.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{qk}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times nd_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{qk}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times nd_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{qk}\in\mathbb{R}^{{\color[rgb]{1,0,0}(d-d_{h})}\times nd_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">d</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">−</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">d</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">h</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{qk}\in\mathbb{R}^{{\color[rgb]{1,0,0}(d-d_{h})}\times nd_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{vo}\in\mathbb{R}^{{\color[rgb]{1,0,0}(d-d_{h})}\times nd_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">d</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">−</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">d</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">h</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{vo}\in\mathbb{R}^{{\color[rgb]{1,0,0}(d-d_{h})}\times nd_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{vo}\in\mathbb{R}^{nd_{h}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{vo}\in\mathbb{R}^{nd_{h}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>; Input <math id="alg2.l0.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times nd_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times nd_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
                
                
               <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}\leftarrow\mathbf{X}\mathbf{B}_{qk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>𝐗𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}\leftarrow\mathbf{X}\mathbf{B}_{qk}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
                
                
               <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbf{K}^{\prime}}\leftarrow{\color[rgb]{1,0,0}{[\mathbf{X}_{:,\,1:d_{h}}]}^{\times n}+\mathbf{X}_{:,\,d_{h}:d}\mathbf{C}_{qk}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">𝐗</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000"> 1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#FF0000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">h</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#FF0000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">n</mi></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">𝐗</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">h</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#FF0000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">d</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">𝐂</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{K}^{\prime}}\leftarrow{\color[rgb]{1,0,0}{[\mathbf{X}_{:,\,1:d_{h}}]}^{\times n}+\mathbf{X}_{:,\,d_{h}:d}\mathbf{C}_{qk}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
                
                
               <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{\prime}\leftarrow{\color[rgb]{1,0,0}{[\mathbf{X}_{:,\,1:d_{h}}]}^{\times n}+\mathbf{X}_{:,\,d_{h}:d}\mathbf{C}_{vo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">𝐗</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000"> 1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#FF0000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">h</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#FF0000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">n</mi></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">𝐗</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">h</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#FF0000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">d</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">𝐂</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{\prime}\leftarrow{\color[rgb]{1,0,0}{[\mathbf{X}_{:,\,1:d_{h}}]}^{\times n}+\mathbf{X}_{:,\,d_{h}:d}\mathbf{C}_{vo}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
                
                
               <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbf{Q}_{1}^{\prime},\dots,\mathbf{Q}_{n}^{\prime}]\leftarrow\mathbf{Q}^{\prime},\;[\mathbf{K}_{1}^{\prime},\dots,\mathbf{K}_{n}^{\prime}]\leftarrow\mathbf{K}^{\prime},\;[\mathbf{V}_{1}^{\prime},\dots,\mathbf{V}_{n}^{\prime}]\leftarrow\mathbf{V}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbf{Q}_{1}^{\prime},\dots,\mathbf{Q}_{n}^{\prime}]\leftarrow\mathbf{Q}^{\prime},\;[\mathbf{K}_{1}^{\prime},\dots,\mathbf{K}_{n}^{\prime}]\leftarrow\mathbf{K}^{\prime},\;[\mathbf{V}_{1}^{\prime},\dots,\mathbf{V}_{n}^{\prime}]\leftarrow\mathbf{V}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
                
                
               <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{O}_{i}^{\prime}\leftarrow\text{softmax}(\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{i}^{\prime}{\mathbf{K}_{i}^{\prime}}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{h}}})\mathbf{V}_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐎</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{O}_{i}^{\prime}\leftarrow\text{softmax}(\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{i}^{\prime}{\mathbf{K}_{i}^{\prime}}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{h}}})\mathbf{V}_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
                
                
               <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}\leftarrow[\mathbf{O}_{1}^{\prime},\dots,\mathbf{O}_{n}^{\prime}]\mathbf{B}_{vo}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐎</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐎</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}\leftarrow[\mathbf{O}_{1}^{\prime},\dots,\mathbf{O}_{n}^{\prime}]\mathbf{B}_{vo}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6a" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
                
                
               <math id="alg2.l6a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<figure id="alg3" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 3</span> </span> BD Attention Preparation (QK)</figcaption>
<div id="alg3.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg3.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
              
              
             <math id="alg3.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{q},\mathbf{W}_{k},\mathbf{W}_{v},\mathbf{W}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{q},\mathbf{W}_{k},\mathbf{W}_{v},\mathbf{W}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> represent query, key, value and output projection matrix; <math id="alg3.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of attention heads

</div>
<div id="alg3.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
              
             <span id="alg3.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg3.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg3.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg3.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              
              Compute first-<math id="alg3.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and last-<math id="alg3.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> residuals for each head of QK with column-based BD: <math id="alg3.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{F}^{i},\mathbf{B}_{F}^{i},\mathbf{C}_{F}^{i},R_{L}^{i},\mathbf{B}_{L}^{i},\mathbf{C}_{L}^{i}\leftarrow\mathrm{BD_{col}}(\mathbf{W}_{q}^{i}{\mathbf{W}_{k}^{i}}^{\top})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>BD</mi><mi>col</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{F}^{i},\mathbf{B}_{F}^{i},\mathbf{C}_{F}^{i},R_{L}^{i},\mathbf{B}_{L}^{i},\mathbf{C}_{L}^{i}\leftarrow\mathrm{BD_{col}}(\mathbf{W}_{q}^{i}{\mathbf{W}_{k}^{i}}^{\top})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
              
             <span id="alg3.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg3.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
              
              
             Compute mean residuals: <math id="alg3.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{R}_{F}\leftarrow\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}R_{F}^{i},\;\bar{R}_{L}\leftarrow\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}R_{L}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>F</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{R}_{F}\leftarrow\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}R_{F}^{i},\;\bar{R}_{L}\leftarrow\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}R_{L}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
              
             <span id="alg3.l5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Select better candidateif</span> <math id="alg3.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{R}_{F}\leq\bar{R}_{\text{last}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>last</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{R}_{F}\leq\bar{R}_{\text{last}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg3.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>
 <span id="alg3.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tag<math id="alg3.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span> <span id="alg3.l5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">First</span>, <math id="alg3.l5.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{i}\leftarrow\mathbf{B}_{F}^{i},\;\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{i}\leftarrow\mathbf{C}_{F}^{i},\;i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{i}\leftarrow\mathbf{B}_{F}^{i},\;\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{i}\leftarrow\mathbf{C}_{F}^{i},\;i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="alg3.l5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>
 <span id="alg3.l5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tag<math id="alg3.l5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span> <span id="alg3.l5.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Last</span>, <math id="alg3.l5.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{i}\leftarrow\mathbf{B}_{L}^{i},\;\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{i}\leftarrow\mathbf{C}_{L}^{i},\;i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{i}\leftarrow\mathbf{B}_{L}^{i},\;\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{i}\leftarrow\mathbf{C}_{L}^{i},\;i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l5a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
              
             <span id="alg3.l5a.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tag</span>, column basis matrices <math id="alg3.l5a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{qk}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (replacement of <math id="alg3.l5a.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{q}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{q}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>), coefficient matrix <math id="alg3.l5a.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{qk}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (replacement of <math id="alg3.l5a.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{k}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{k}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="alg3.l5a.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Preservation of query–key similarity.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The transformed projections <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}_{i}{\mathbf{K}^{\prime}_{i}}^{\top}=\mathbf{Q}_{i}\mathbf{K}_{i}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}_{i}{\mathbf{K}^{\prime}_{i}}^{\top}=\mathbf{Q}_{i}\mathbf{K}_{i}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, which means that every pairwise inner product between queries and keys is exactly preserved.
<math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can be regarded as an <em id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inner-product isomorphic</em> representation of the original <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{i},\mathbf{K}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{i},\mathbf{K}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in a <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space.
We therefore still denote them as queries and keys (<math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}_{i},\mathbf{K}^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}_{i},\mathbf{K}^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) to emphasize that they still maintain the essential property of attention: query–key similarity.
Advanced compression methods relying on query–key similarity, such as KV-cache compression, remain fully compatible with BDA.
Since the inner products are exactly preserved, these methods can be seamlessly integrated with BDA, enabling it to serve as a general and complementary acceleration framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate BD Attention (BDA) from three perspectives: inference accuracy and efficiency, training evaluation, and integration with low-rank pruning. All experiments are conducted on NVIDIA A6000.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01718v1/ppl_diff.svg" id="S4.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:236/176;" width="236" height="176"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span> End-to-end accuracy</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01718v1/speedup.svg" id="S4.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:285/177;" width="285" height="177"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span> Efficiency</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S4.F2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation of BD Attention (BDA).</span>
<span id="S4.F2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a) End-to-end accuracy</span>: Perplexity (<math id="S4.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>) increase on WikiText2 when replacing all MHA layers of DeepSeek-V2-Lite with BDA.
The increase is nearly imperceptible <span id="S4.F2.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.02% (FP16)</span>, with <span id="S4.F2.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Residual-min</span> performing better. For reference, the dashed line shows the degradation from a structured pruning baseline at the same compression ratio (<math id="S4.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\%</annotation></semantics></math> K/V channels).
<span id="S4.F2.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b) Efficiency</span>: Relative speedup for the <math id="S4.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\_proj" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\_proj</annotation></semantics></math> operator under FP16 and BF16. The dashed line at <math id="S4.F2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.33\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.33</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.33\times</annotation></semantics></math> marks the theoretical bound. Measured speedups fluctuate around this line but consistently exceed the MHA baseline, averaging <span id="S4.F2.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.32<math id="S4.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></span> (FP16) and <span id="S4.F2.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.34<math id="S4.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></span> (BF16). BDA also reduces parameter and memory usage by <span id="S4.F2.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">25%</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>BDA Inference: Accuracy and Efficiency</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Accuracy</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F2.sf1" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a> reports the increase in perplexity on WikiText2 when replacing all MHA layers in DeepSeek-V2-Lite (16B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> with BDA.
Two strategies are compared: <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">First-<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which always selects the first <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> rows, and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Residual-min</span>, which adaptively selects between the first or last <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> rows depending on the smaller reconstruction residual.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The perplexity increase is nearly imperceptible <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.0004% (FP32), 0.02% (FP16), 0.2% (BF16)</span>, with <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Residual-min</span> consistently outperforming <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">First-<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math></span>.
A similar advantage is observed at the operator level in per-layer reconstruction errors (see Appendix Table <a href="#A5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix E LLM Usage ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), where <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Residual-min</span> achieves up to an order-of-magnitude lower error in FP32.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For reference, we also include a structured pruning baseline that removes <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\%</annotation></semantics></math> of K/V channels at the same compression ratio (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{h}/d=128/512=25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>128</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{h}/d=128/512=25\%</annotation></semantics></math>).
This baseline follows the relative-importance scoring strategy of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a></cite>, where each channel’s importance is estimated, summed, and the least important <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\%</annotation></semantics></math> are pruned.
Although more recent structured pruning techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">van der Ouderaa et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ashkboos et al., 2024</a>)</cite> achieve better performance, they typically require access to a calibration dataset, which is beyond the scope of this comparison.
Here, structured pruning is reported only as a reference for the scale of perplexity degradation at the same compression ratio.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Efficiency</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt the DeepSeek-V3 KV configuration (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=512</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{h}=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{h}=128</annotation></semantics></math>), where the compression ratio is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{h}/d=25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{h}/d=25\%</annotation></semantics></math>. This ratio is identical across DeepSeek-V2 Lite (16B), DeepSeek-V2 (236B), and DeepSeek-V3 (660B), so the reported results generalize consistently across all these models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To maximize efficiency, we implement a custom Triton kernel for BDA that fuses the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">slice</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">repeat</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">matrix multiplication</span>, and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">matrix addition</span> steps of the k_proj operator (Line 2 of Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3.4 BD for Multi-Head Attention ‣ 3 Method ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) into a single kernel, thereby reducing redundant memory I/O.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For comparison, we also construct a <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PIFA-style attention</span> variant: for each head <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we run QR with column pivoting on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\,\mathbf{W}_{Q}^{i}(\mathbf{W}_{K}^{i})^{\top}\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\,\mathbf{W}_{Q}^{i}(\mathbf{W}_{K}^{i})^{\top}\,</annotation></semantics></math> to select pivot rows (akin to the PIFA layer of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2025)</a></cite>), then reconstruct the remaining rows via its coefficient matrix. This per-head pivoting yields different basis across heads and thus forces per-head copies and slices of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, substantially increasing memory traffic. Consequently, <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">PIFA-style attention</em> is slower than even baseline MHA (Table <a href="#A5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix E LLM Usage ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, <a href="#A5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Appendix E LLM Usage ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). In contrast, BDA aligns all heads to a shared contiguous basis (all first-<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> or last-<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>), enabling a single shared <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and coalesced memory access; with our fused Triton kernel, BDA attains near-theoretical speedups (Fig. <a href="#S4.F2.sf2" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2b</span></a>), averaging <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.32<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></span> (FP16) and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.34<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></span> (BF16).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="S4.T2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training evaluation of BD Attention (BDA).</span>
BLEU scores (<math id="S4.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>) on the IWSLT’14 using the Transformer model. The columns correspond to the <span id="S4.T2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LR scale</span>, i.e., the multiplicative factor applied to the learning rate of <span id="S4.T2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Noam</span> schedule <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. Across all scales, BDA achieves <span id="S4.T2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">comparable</span> BLEU scores to MHA, despite not guaranteeing identical gradients, and requires no hyperparameter tuning. <span id="S4.T2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span> numbers indicate the higher BLEU for each LR scale.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T2.12" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T2.12.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.12.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"></th>
<th id="S4.T2.12.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">LR Scale=0.5</th>
<th id="S4.T2.12.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">LR Scale=1</th>
<th id="S4.T2.12.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">LR Scale=2</th>
<th id="S4.T2.12.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">LR Scale=4</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.12.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.12.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">MHA</th>
<td id="S4.T2.12.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">24.98</td>
<td id="S4.T2.12.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">23.98</td>
<td id="S4.T2.12.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">23.86</td>
<td id="S4.T2.12.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">24.04</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.12.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.12.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">BDA</th>
<td id="S4.T2.12.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.12.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">25.27</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.12.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.12.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">25.04</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.12.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.12.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">23.89</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.12.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.12.3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">24.14</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>BDA Training Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike inference, where BDA is mathematically lossless and produces outputs identical to MHA, training dynamics can differ because gradients are not guaranteed to match exactly.
To evaluate this, we trained Transformer models on the IWSLT’14 English-to-German <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cettolo et al., 2014</a>)</cite> using either standard MHA or BDA as the attention module, both under the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Noam</span> learning-rate schedule <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite>.
We swept across four learning-rate scales (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5,1,2,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5,1,2,4</annotation></semantics></math>).
As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Efficiency ‣ 4.1 BDA Inference: Accuracy and Efficiency ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, despite potential differences in optimization dynamics, the final BLEU scores of BDA are <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">comparable</span> to those of MHA across all settings.
All hyperparameters are detailed in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Hyperparameters for BDA Training ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, and were kept identical between MHA and BDA.
This highlights that BDA requires no hyperparameter search or tuning, and thus can be <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">seamlessly integrated</span> into existing training pipelines without additional cost, while maintaining model quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>BD for Low-Rank Pruning</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate BD when applied on top of models already compressed by low-rank pruning (Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 BD for Linear Layer ‣ 3 Method ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3 BD for Low-Rank Pruning ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Accelerating Attention with Basis Decomposition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> reports results on LLaMA2-7B and LLaMA2-13B under three settings:
(i) <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dense</span>, the original pretrained LLaMA2 model;
(ii) <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low-rank (80% density)</span>, where weights are pruned into a low-rank structure following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2025)</a></cite>;
and (iii) <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BD (from low-rank)</span>, where the pruned low-rank weights are further transformed using Basis Decomposition (BD).
No retraining is performed in any case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Results show that BD consistently improves efficiency over the low-rank baseline while preserving perplexity.
On average, BD increases throughput by <span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">17.21%</span> and reduces memory usage by <span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">16.52%</span> compared to low-rank models,
while keeping perplexity nearly unchanged.
This demonstrates that BD is complementary to existing compression techniques and can serve as a plug-in acceleration step for low-rank pruned models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span><span id="S4.T3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BD applied to low-rank pruning</span> on LLaMA2 models (FP16).
BD further improves throughput and memory efficiency over low-rank pruning while preserving perplexity.
Best throughput and memory are highlighted in <span id="S4.T3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>.</figcaption>
